PR Contributor Declaration (checker landed, rollout pending):

The action, the reusable workflow and this repo's own template are in. What
remains is turning it on across the org, in this order -- reversing steps 3 and
4 fails contributors who faithfully used their own repo's template, because a
template change only affects NEW pull requests while every already-open one
keeps its old body.

1. add the standard consumer caller to this repo:
   .github/workflows/contributor-declaration.yml, exactly the README snippet.
   It must reference
   ecmwf/ci-infrastructure/.github/workflows/check-pr-declaration.yml@main, so
   it cannot be committed until the workflow is on main -- hence a follow-up
   rather than part of the original commit. pr-declaration-dogfood.yml is not a
   substitute: it runs on `pull_request` so that a PR is judged by its own
   version of the checker, which is the right trade for developing the action
   and the wrong one for enforcement.

2. measure the blast radius before requiring anything anywhere. Run the checker
   over every open pull request in the org
   (`gh api search/issues -f q='org:ecmwf is:pr is:open'`, ~1036 of them) and
   publish the would-fail list. 212 are dependabot's and exempt by default; the
   rest is the number to understand. There is deliberately no warn-only mode, so
   this scratch run is the only way to see the impact before it is real.

3. converge the drifting templates. These ship a one-line variant ("...I affirm
   that all authors agree to the [Contributor License Agreement.](...)") which
   the canonical block rejects: anemoi, anemoi-core, anemoi-datasets,
   anemoi-utils, anemoi-inference, anemoi-registry, ellem. Either replace the
   file with the org block or delete it so the org template applies. A repo
   mid-migration can point the check at its own copy with
   `declaration-file: .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`, but that is a bridge,
   not a destination -- the point is one text org-wide.

4. add the caller workflow per repo, after its template is fixed.

5. only then require the context, and prefer an org-level repository ruleset
   ("Require workflows to pass before merging") pointing at a SHA-pinned
   ecmwf/ci-infrastructure/.github/workflows/check-pr-declaration.yml over
   hand-adding a required context string per repo. One config instead of N,
   GitHub owns the triggering (so a caller that forgets `edited` cannot silently
   disable the gate), and the pinned workflow is not editable by the pull
   request under test. While in there, consider a ruleset path restriction on
   .github/workflows/** so PRs cannot touch workflow files without a bypass
   actor.

Two gaps that need a merge-time check, both documented in the action's
description. Neither is closable at pull_request_target time:

- merge queues. A pull_request_target workflow never runs on `merge_group`, so a
  repo that enables a merge queue would have entries wait for a check that never
  arrives, time out (60 min default) and be dequeued -- every PR in that repo
  unmergeable. Extend the gate BEFORE any repo turns a queue on: parse the PR
  number out of refs/heads/gh-readonly-queue/<base>/pr-<N>-<sha> and read the
  body with `body-source: api`, since there is no pull_request payload there.

- a description edited with GITHUB_TOKEN does not start a new workflow run, so
  an automation calling `gh pr edit --body ''` leaves the earlier green result
  standing. Wants either the merge_group leg above or a post-merge audit on
  `push: main` that re-reads the merged PR's body and opens an issue on
  mismatch.


